    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d189">

                <pb facs="0241" n="231" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg231"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7–147: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Stuart</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-07-10T19:00:00-04:00">July 10, 1947—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>850. Following precedent set in your conversation with Generalissimo re
                    conditions in Formosa,<anchor n="23" xml:id="d189-f1"/> suggest you communicate
                    orally to <pb facs="0242" n="232" xml:id="pg232"/> Generalissimo observations
                    made in Mukden’s 189 June 23<anchor n="24" xml:id="d189-f2"/> (beginning para 5
                    “Nationalist southern military” and ending para 8 “into intramural China”).
                    Without associating yourself with views expressed, you may say that info comes
                    to you from responsible confidential observers in Manchuria.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d189-f1" n="23" xml:id="d189-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram No. 689, March 29, 5 p.m., from the Ambassador in China, <ref
